{"state": {"case_id": "MB_7", "timestep": 4, "case_summary": "A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with worsening dyspnea over the past 48 hours. He recently had a cold that kept him home from work for the past week. He has a past medical history of diabetes, obesity, and hypertension. He had his Achilles tendon repaired 4 weeks ago and he has been less mobile. His temperature is 99.2°F (37.3°C), blood pressure is 150/85 mmHg, pulse is 82/min, respirations are 16/min, and oxygen saturation is 100% on room air. Physical exam is notable for dyspnea provoked by walking short distances. Jugular venous distension is noted on exam. The patient’s blood pressure is 130/70 mmHg during inspiration. A bedside echocardiogram demonstrates impaired diastolic filling with normal ventricular contractility. An ECG is performed as seen in Figure A. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Cardiac tamponade\nB. Constrictive pericarditis\nC. Myocardial infarction\nD. Pulmonary embolism\nE. Restrictive cardiomyopathy\n", "root": {"label": "Subacute Progressive Dyspnea with Pulsus Paradoxus and Uncharacterised Pericardial or ECG Abnormality", "time_course": "subacute", "severity": "unspecified", "confidence": 0.5, "supporting_facts": ["worsening dyspnea over 48 hours", "Beck sign variant (inspiratory systolic BP drop >10 mmHg to 130/70)", "jugular venous distension", "impaired diastolic filling on echocardiogram", "recent cold and surgical history (Achilles repair 4 weeks ago)"], "excluded_candidates": ["Pulmonary Embolism: Immobility post-Achilles repair increases risk, but clinical findings prioritize pericardial pathology", "Restrictive Cardiomyopathy: Could explain diastolic impairment, but lacks acute-onset signs"], "alarm_features": ["Beck sign variant (pulsus paradoxus >10 mmHg)", "jugular venous distension (elevated venous pressure)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Acute Pericardial Effusion with Tamponade", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.39819004524886875, "posterior": 0.4249079008548231, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The acute-onset dyspnea and significant inspiratory BP drop (Beck sign variant) increase suspicion for acute pericardial effusion (tamponade) rather than chronic pericardial disease.", "Chronic symptoms such as ascites or night sweats are typical of constrictive pericarditis, making their absence a negative predictor for this diagnosis."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there a history of recent viral infection or surgery?", "Are there signs of hemodynamic instability (syncope, hypotension)?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Repeat bedside echocardiogram for pericardial effusion size and tamponade signs", "Chest X-ray for signs of pericardial effusion (widespread meniscus sign)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Chronic Pericardial Constriction", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.0509674881075861, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Chronic symptoms such as ascites or night sweats are typical of constrictive pericarditis, making their absence a negative predictor for this diagnosis."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Acute Coronary Obstruction with Diastolic Dysfunction", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.02548374405379305, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The acute-onset dyspnea and significant inspiratory BP drop (Beck sign variant) increase suspicion for acute pericardial effusion (tamponade) rather than chronic pericardial disease."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Thromboembolic Pulmonary Disease with Right Heart Strain", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.05436532064809184, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The subacute 48-hour onset does not completely exclude thromboembolic pulmonary disease (B4), but is atypical for acute embolic events. This marginally reduces B4's likelihood."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Restrictive Cardiac Conditions with Diastolic Impairment", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.00849458135126435, "danger": 0.55, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Chronic symptoms such as ascites or night sweats are typical of constrictive pericarditis, making their absence a negative predictor for this diagnosis."]}, "B6": {"id": "B6", "label": "Reactive Inflammatory Cardiac Conditions", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.010873064129618368, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Missing typical viral pericarditis symptoms (e.g., chest pain alleviated by sitting forward) weakens support for viral-induced tamponade (B1.1) and reactive inflammatory pericardial disease (B6), though does not fully exclude them."]}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "Viral-Induced Acute Pericardial Effusion with Tamponade", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.1802639579384098, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The recent cold increases suspicion of viral-induced pericardial effusion (B1.1), a common cause of acute pericardial disease. However, without chest pain relieved by sitting forward (a hallmark of viral pericarditis), this evidence remains inconclusive."], "evidence_against": ["Classic signs of viral pericarditis, such as chest pain alleviated by sitting forward, are absent in this patient. This weakens but does not exclude viral etiology (B1.1), necessitating further assessment.", "Missing typical viral pericarditis symptoms (e.g., chest pain alleviated by sitting forward) weakens support for viral-induced tamponade (B1.1) and reactive inflammatory pericardial disease (B6), though does not fully exclude them."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1.1", "B1.1.2", "B1.1.3", "B1.1.4"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the pericardial effusion correlate with recent upper respiratory infection (cold)?", "Are there symptoms of pericarditis, such as chest pain, which is relieved by sitting forward?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["PCR for EBV/CMV", "Pericardial fluid analysis for lymphocytic predominance"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "Post-Surgical Pericardial Effusion with Tamponade", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.1802639579384098, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The subacute onset (4 weeks post-surgery) and immobilization increase the plausibility of post-surgical pericardial effusion (B1.2). Surgical pericardial complications can manifest later due to delayed fluid accumulation."], "evidence_against": ["The surgical history lacks direct evidence of pericardial injury or retrograde stasis, which are critical for post-surgical effusion diagnosis (B1.2). This weakens the argument for this sub-branch despite temporal correlation."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.2.1", "B1.2.2", "B1.2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the effusion occur within 6 weeks of Achilles tendon surgery?", "Is there evidence of retrograde venous stasis or pericardial injury in surgical history?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Doppler ultrasound for postoperative thrombosis", "CT chest with contrast to evaluate surgical complication"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.3, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.85, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "management_pathway"}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "Neoplastic Pericardial Effusion with Tamponade", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.060331825037707384, "posterior": 0.0643799849780035, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["The absence of systemic malignancy signs (weight loss, night sweats) contradicts neoplastic pericardial effusion (B1.3), though ECG findings (low voltage in Figure A) are not explicitly described in the text to confirm or refute this.", "A known malignancy would strongly implicate neoplastic effusion (B1.3), but its absence here supports less ominous subacute causes like viral or post-operative etiologies. This weakens neoplastic pericardial disease as a dominant differentiator."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does the patient have a history of known malignancy or paraneoplastic syndromes?", "Are there signs of systemic illness (weight loss, night sweats) alongside effusion?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CT/PET for malignancy screening", "Pericardial fluid cytology or tumor markers"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.8, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B1.1.1": {"id": "B1.1.1", "label": "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) Induced Pericardial Tamponade", "parent": "B1.1", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04506598948460245, "posterior": 0.04506598948460245, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Does EBV serology in the context of pericarditis align with recent upper respiratory infection?", "Was the recent cold a known EBV reactivation in this patient?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["EBV DNA PCR in serum/pericardial fluid", "EBV antibody panel (VCA, EA, EBNA)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.1, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B1.1.2": {"id": "B1.1.2", "label": "Cytomegalovirus (CMV) Induced Pericardial Tamponade", "parent": "B1.1", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04506598948460245, "posterior": 0.04506598948460245, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is the patient immunocompromised (e.g., diabetes, obesity-related metabolic dysfunction) which would heighten CMV suspicion?", "Does the pericardial fluid analysis show high neutrophil counts typical of CMV?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["CMV pp65 antigenemia testing", "CMV DNA PCR in pericardial fluid"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.3, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "management_pathway"}, "B1.1.3": {"id": "B1.1.3", "label": "Coxsackievirus Induced Pericardial Tamponade", "parent": "B1.1", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04506598948460245, "posterior": 0.04506598948460245, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Are there concurrent signs of myocarditis (e.g., chest pain, arrhythmias) which is common with Coxsackie etiology?", "Did chest X-ray show pericardial calcifications arguing against viral etiology?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Coxsackie B viral culture or PCR", "Pericardial fluid lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) levels"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.4, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "urgence"}, "B1.1.4": {"id": "B1.1.4", "label": "Parvovirus B19 Induced Pericardial Tamponade", "parent": "B1.1", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.04506598948460245, "posterior": 0.04506598948460245, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there a history of prolonged fever (weeks) typical of parvovirus pericarditis?", "Are there associated symptoms of arthritis or rash suggesting parvovirus?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Parvovirus B19 PCR", "Pericardial fluid viral load testing"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.35, "interrupt_relevance": 0.65, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B1.2.1": {"id": "B1.2.1", "label": "Postoperative Dressler Syndrome (Autoimmune Pericarditis)", "parent": "B1.2", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.06008798597946993, "posterior": 0.06008798597946993, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there a history of recent pericardial or myocardial injury (e.g., cardiac surgery, trauma) that could trigger autoimmune pathophysiology?", "Are there elevated inflammatory markers (ESR, CRP) or evidence of antinuclear antibodies?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["C-reactive protein (CRP) and erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR)", "Autoimmune panel (ANA, ANCA, etc.)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.65, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B1.2.2": {"id": "B1.2.2", "label": "Postoperative Septic Pericarditis from Distant Surgical Infection", "parent": "B1.2", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.06008798597946993, "posterior": 0.06008798597946993, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is there evidence of sepsis from the surgical site (e.g., wound infection, leukocytosis)?", "Was surgical drainage or culture performed during Achilles repair showing infection?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Blood cultures", "Pericardial fluid Gram stain and culture"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.1, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.55, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "test_pathway"}, "B1.2.3": {"id": "B1.2.3", "label": "Postoperative Hemorrhagic Effusion from Anticoagulation Complication", "parent": "B1.2", "level": 3, "status": "live", "prior": 0.06008798597946993, "posterior": 0.06008798597946993, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Is the patient on anticoagulants (e.g., LMWH) post-surgery that might contribute to bleeding into the pericardium?", "Do coagulation studies show abnormalities (INR, PT/PTT) in this context?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Coagulation profile (PT/INR, APTT)", "Pericardial fluid cell count and biochemistry (elevated RBC: hemorrhagic effusion)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.3, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.65, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "management_pathway"}}, "frontier": ["B1.3", "B1.1.1", "B1.1.2", "B1.1.3", "B1.1.4", "B1.2.1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of a recent viral cold align with EBV’s known association with post-viral pericarditis in this patient’s context?", "result_summary": ""}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the diabetes and obesity in this patient increase the likelihood of CMV reactivation as a cause of pericardial tamponade?", "result_summary": ""}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the 4-week timeframe from surgery to symptoms align with postoperative Dressler syndrome (autoimmune pericarditis) despite the lack of retrograde venous stasis evidence?", "result_summary": ""}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the lack of documented systemic malignancy or paraneoplastic symptoms (e.g., night sweats) sufficient to rule out neoplastic pericardial effusion (B1.3)?", "result_summary": ""}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of chest pain or arrhythmias (critical Coxsackie features) in a patient with impaired diastolic filling disfavour this sub-branch (B1.1.3) despite the recent viral syndrome?", "result_summary": ""}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is a 48-hour symptomatic progression more consistent with parvovirus B19 pericarditis (chronic/indolent), or a different etiology given the acute onset?", "result_summary": ""}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.6728971962616823, "B2": 0.14018691588785046, "B3": 0.07009345794392523, "B4": 0.07476635514018691, "B5": 0.02336448598130841, "B6": 0.018691588785046728, "B1.1": 0.24790949335956716, "B1.2": 0.24790949335956716, "B1.3": 0.17707820954254797}, {"B1": 0.39819004524886875, "B2": 0.04776269482151834, "B3": 0.02388134741075917, "B4": 0.05094687447628623, "B5": 0.007960449136919723, "B6": 0.012736718619071557, "B1.1": 0.16892911010558068, "B1.2": 0.16892911010558068, "B1.3": 0.060331825037707384}, {"B1": 0.0643799849780035, "B2": 0.0509674881075861, "B3": 0.02548374405379305, "B4": 0.05436532064809184, "B5": 0.00849458135126435, "B6": 0.010873064129618368, "B1.1": 0.1802639579384098, "B1.2": 0.1802639579384098, "B1.3": 0.0643799849780035, "B1.1.1": 0.04506598948460245, "B1.1.2": 0.04506598948460245, "B1.1.3": 0.04506598948460245, "B1.1.4": 0.04506598948460245, "B1.2.1": 0.06008798597946993, "B1.2.2": 0.06008798597946993, "B1.2.3": 0.06008798597946993}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Cardiac tamponade is a time-critical syndrome requiring immediate intervention. Signs of Beck variant (pulsus paradoxus >10mmHg) suggest hemodynamic compromise. Impaired diastolic filling on echocardiogram confirms pericardial pathology.", "required_actions": ["Perform emergent pericardiocentesis", "Start IV crystalloids to increase preload"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "[static mode override] Active cardiac tamponade (Beck sign variant + impaired diastolic filling on echo) requires urgent pericardiocentesis. Delaying intervention to refine etiology (B1.1/B1.2/B1.3) is unsafe due to critical hemodynamic compromise. — emergency_override suppressed in static_diagnosis_qa mode"}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.25, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.0643799849780035, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 55-year-old man presents to the emergency department with worsening dyspnea over the past 48 hours. He recently had a cold that kept him home from work for the past week. He has a past medical history of diabetes, obesity, and hypertension. He had his Achilles tendon repaired 4 weeks ago and he has been less mobile. His temperature is 99.2°F (37.3°C), blood pressure is 150/85 mmHg, pulse is 82/min, respirations are 16/min, and oxygen saturation is 100% on room air. Physical exam is notable for dyspnea provoked by walking short distances. Jugular venous distension is noted on exam. The patient’s blood pressure is 130/70 mmHg during inspiration. A bedside echocardiogram demonstrates impaired diastolic filling with normal ventricular contractility. An ECG is performed as seen in Figure A.", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Cardiac tamponade"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Constrictive pericarditis"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Myocardial infarction"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Pulmonary embolism"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Restrictive cardiomyopathy"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Patient: 55-year-old man", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Chief complaint: worsening dyspnea for 48 hours", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Recent cold: home from work for previous week", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "k